2026-10-17T21:43:56.3082017Z FAIL target identity change during staging is refused status=created detail="refreshed marker-owned region" target="<!-- nana:working-under-nana-pi begin -->\n## Working under nana-pi\n\nThe bullets below describe pi sessions; other runtimes receive only the surfaces listed here. At startup, read `HANDOFF.md`, look up affected `REQUIREMENTS.md` rows by ID (grep or the requirements skill), and read the landscape doc only for pi API questions.\n\n| Runtime | Objective | Shared memory | nana-soul / nana-standards | Writing rule | Knowledge pull | Gate | Verifier pipe | Post-edit | Compaction summary | Notify |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Claude Code seat | SessionStart `nana-objective.mjs` and `nana-adoption.mjs` hooks | `nana-shared-memory.mjs` hook; Claude shared-memory index and auto-memory | Both Claude rules | Shared nana-writing rule | UserPromptSubmit `nana-knowledge.ts hook` | No nana command gate | PreToolUse Node hook `verifier-pipe.mjs` (matcher `Bash` on macOS and Linux, `Bash\\|PowerShell` on Windows) | No nana per-edit checks | Claude-owned summary; no nana HANDOFF producer | No nana notify; SessionStart `nana-roster-notice.mjs` hook shows the roster notice |\n| pi TUI or desk session | `nana-objective` extension | No shared auto-memory | Neither rule; requirements-first arrives through AGENTS and the requirements skill | `nana-writing` extension | `nana-knowledge` extension (`before_agent_start`) | `nana-gate` extension | `nana-gate` shared predicate for bash and PowerShell | `nana-post-edit` extension; configured checks, if any | `nana-lifecycle` journal; `nana-handoff` extension on compaction | `nana-notify` extension; `nana-roster-notice` extension shows the roster notice as a widget |\n| pi reviewer/worker child (`NANA_HANDOFF=off`) | `nana-objective` extension | No shared auto-memory | Neither rule | `nana-writing` extension | Disabled by `NANA_ROLE=reviewer` or `NANA_ROLE=worker` | `nana-gate` extension | `nana-gate` shared predicate for bash and PowerShell | `nana-post-edit` extension; configured checks, if any | Disabled by `NANA_HANDOFF=off` | `nana-notify` extension; `nana-roster-notice` extension runs but shows nothing (no UI) |\n| Codex | Unsupported; no nana runtime contract | Not specified | Not specified | Not specified | Not specified | Not specified | Not specified | Not specified | Not specified | Not specified |\n\npi deliberately has no user-level `AGENTS.md`; shared auto-memory and nana-soul are Claude-only. Requirements-first practice is shared through project `AGENTS.md` and the requirements skill. The separate nana-knowledge extension serves pi; Claude Code has its own knowledge hook.\n\n### pi-session guidance\n\n- **Objective:** nana-objective supplies the nearest project objective unless disabled by user configuration; the shared rules above describe pi-only behavior.\n- **Compaction summary:** compaction summaries are pi-owned background state, not the project frontier. Persist unresolved work and Jake's open questions in `HANDOFF.md` before a final report or session boundary.\n- **Journal and notify:** journal records session events; notify is a pi extension and may be disabled or silent headlessly.\n- **Gate:** Advisory pi gate inspects selected shell commands and protected edit/write targets. It is not a security boundary; see the pack README for exact scope and limits, including the interpreter code-operand path gap for floored settings, auth, MCP and extension files.\n- **Post-edit:** configured checks run after successful edits only in pi; absent commands mean no checks. Claude Code has no nana per-edit checks.\n\n### Working pattern\n\n- Default worktree root: `~/<repo>-wt/<lane>`; branch: `feat/<lane>`, based on main. One writer per worktree.\n- Review corpus: `docs/reviews/<lane>-<date>/`. Builder launcher: `pi-worker --lane <name> --brief <file> --out <file>`; reviewer launcher: `pi-review`.\n- Review ladder: an ordinary lane gets one final reviewer, the roster's reviewer. An install, hook or gate lane also gets the land reviewer, the seat's wrong-code pairs and one independent check. An install, hook or gate lane changes nana-setup install, uninstall or doctor; a Claude Code hook or a pi extension; the nana-gate policy; or the land and review tools (`nana-land`, `pi-review`, `review-round`). Every other lane is ordinary. Maximum three rounds per item; a different model lineage reviews the work. The final review must cover the exact tip that will land; every later commit needs its own accepted review coverage.\n- Plans: a lane is one feature. The seat and one adversarial agent write its must-meet tests, about three to eight, one requirement row each, and the builder builds to them. Every plan names its threat model: honest variation in our tools' output is in scope, deliberate forgery is out, and a finding outside the model is a residual. Plans get no review round. Urgent fixes ship alone and are hardened in a later lane. Once every reviewer says LAND, nothing more is edited.\n- Review bar: `pi-review` appends the review standard to every review it runs, so its briefs state the threat model and lane class but not the bar. A reviewer sends back only for a bug that blocks the feature or a harm the standard lists; every other finding is a known bug, recorded in HANDOFF's residuals and fixed when someone hits it or next works on its feature. A review with no MUST finding says LAND; LAND-WITH-FIXES is retired because `nana-land` accepts only LAND. A confirmation round reviews only the change since the last round, and its brief names that revision.\n- Land checklist: resolve review findings or record residuals at point of use; use `nana-land` with a clean `main` checkout and clean source, verified review rounds, and the suite on the reviewed tip; rerun the suite if either checkout changes, and rely on the helper's immediate pre-merge checks, ff-only merge, and containment verification. Every commit reaching main must be covered by a revision every reviewer accepted; there are no exemptions, and never commit directly to main. Put HANDOFF and session notes in the reviewed change before its final round; nothing is written after a land. For a confirmation round over cap, use the standard reason: `confirmation round on the final tip after fixes to named findings or a merge of current main`. Cleanup is a separate operation, only for a clean `feat/<lane>` worktree contained in `main`. Then run map, README, and locked full-suite checks; commit explicit paths and report evidence.\n- Default pattern: a change that touches code with tests goes through the pattern; questions and one-line fixes get a direct answer. The builder, reviewer and land models come from this machine's roster, printed by `node \"$HOME/nana-pi/packages/nana-pack/bin/nana-roster.mjs\"`. Where pi-worker and pi-review are not on PATH (Windows), run them as `node \"$HOME/nana-pi/packages/nana-pack/bin/pi-worker.mjs\"` and `node \"$HOME/nana-pi/packages/nana-pack/bin/pi-review.mjs\"` with the same arguments.\n- Seat land review: in an install, hook or gate lane, when the roster sets no land model (it prints `land: seat`), the seat writes its land review as `docs/reviews/<lane>-<date>/seat-land.md` in the lane's reviewed change before the final round; the independent slice review still covers every land.\n- Larger lanes: when the roster sets `largerBuilder`, a seat builds a larger or riskier lane with it: a pi entry through `pi-worker --lane-size larger`, a Claude Code entry as a Claude Code subagent in the lane worktree; a small lane goes through `pi-worker --lane-size small`. A seat that cannot start that builder stops and asks the user rather than starting another. With no `largerBuilder`, the builder builds every lane.\n- Seat guide: while a builder or reviewer runs, the seat does not read or judge its partial work, and it acts when the run completes. The full guide is `$HOME/nana-pi/packages/nana-setup/claude/rules/nana-seat.md`; Claude Code loads it as a rule.\n- Project-specific overrides belong in that project's own section AFTER this marker region; `nana-setup project` replaces everything between the markers.\n<!-- nana:working-under-nana-pi end -->\n"
